1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,883
Welcome to the Modern Java Enterprise Handbook.

2
00:00:03,883 --> 00:00:07,725
This is chapter thirty-six: startup and Project Leyden.

3
00:00:08,175 --> 00:00:12,384
Java's slow start was fine when a JVM ran for months.

4
00:00:12,384 --> 00:00:21,039
It stopped being fine when JVMs started launching per autoscaling event, per CI job, and per serverless call.

5
00:00:21,939 --> 00:00:25,425
There are two separate numbers that get conflated.

6
00:00:25,425 --> 00:00:29,329
Startup: three point three seconds to the first request.

7
00:00:29,329 --> 00:00:35,742
Warmup: another thirty-five seconds to peak throughput, while the JIT profiles and compiles.

8
00:00:36,192 --> 00:00:39,165
Which one hurts depends on how you deploy.

9
00:00:39,165 --> 00:00:42,139
A long-running service with rare restarts?

10
00:00:42,139 --> 00:00:42,705
Neither.

11
00:00:42,705 --> 00:00:43,979
Skip this chapter.

12
00:00:43,979 --> 00:00:45,749
Autoscaling under bursts?

13
00:00:45,749 --> 00:00:50,280
Both, because new instances are slow exactly when you need them.

14
00:00:50,280 --> 00:00:52,758
Serverless is dominated by startup.

15
00:00:52,758 --> 00:00:58,421
And a thirty-second batch job never finishes warming up, so it runs interpreted.

16
00:00:59,321 --> 00:01:02,152
Each mitigation targets a different phase.

17
00:01:02,152 --> 00:01:09,497
Class loading and verification, reading and checking thousands of classes, is attacked by class data sharing.

18
00:01:09,947 --> 00:01:15,387
Linking and initialisation is attacked by the AOT cache from Project Leyden.

19
00:01:15,837 --> 00:01:21,875
The slow climb from interpreter to compiled code is attacked by recorded method profiles.

20
00:01:22,325 --> 00:01:27,253
And native image and CRaC attack all of it, in very different ways.

21
00:01:27,703 --> 00:01:34,714
The key asymmetry: CDS and the AOT cache change nothing about how your application behaves.

22
00:01:34,714 --> 00:01:38,412
Native image changes the execution model itself.

23
00:01:39,312 --> 00:01:43,060
Before choosing anything, measure which phase dominates.

24
00:01:43,060 --> 00:01:47,611
Here, loading classes is over two seconds of a three-second startup.

25
00:01:48,061 --> 00:01:53,024
If class loading dominates, CDS and the AOT cache are the answer.

26
00:01:53,024 --> 00:02:03,409
If application initialisation dominates, like building caches, scanning the classpath or opening connection pools, no JVM feature helps.

27
00:02:03,409 --> 00:02:05,853
The work is in your application.

28
00:02:06,753 --> 00:02:14,446
Class data sharing stores classes pre-parsed and pre-verified, memory-mapped straight into the JVM.

29
00:02:14,446 --> 00:02:18,177
JDK classes are shared by default since Java 12.

30
00:02:18,177 --> 00:02:28,513
For your own classes, since Java 19, one flag creates the archive on first run, uses it after, and regenerates it if the jar changes.

31
00:02:28,963 --> 00:02:34,457
In containers, build the archive at image build time so it ships in a layer.

32
00:02:34,457 --> 00:02:41,035
It must be built on the same JDK version that uses it, so a base image bump invalidates it.

33
00:02:41,035 --> 00:02:44,216
Another reason to build it inside the image.

34
00:02:45,116 --> 00:02:47,018
The AOT cache goes further.

35
00:02:47,018 --> 00:02:51,668
First, a training run records what was actually loaded and linked.

36
00:02:51,668 --> 00:03:00,898
The training run is what matters: one that only starts and exits captures nothing about the request path, so run a real smoke test.

37
00:03:00,898 --> 00:03:02,588
Second, build the cache.

38
00:03:02,588 --> 00:03:04,913
Third, run with it in production.

39
00:03:05,363 --> 00:03:08,303
Classes come pre-loaded and pre-linked.

40
00:03:08,303 --> 00:03:17,950
And with Java 25's method profiling, the cache also carries JIT profile data, so the compiler can skip straight to higher tiers.

41
00:03:17,950 --> 00:03:20,814
That attacks warmup, not just startup.

42
00:03:20,814 --> 00:03:28,275
Any JDK, classpath or application change invalidates it, so build it in CI as part of the artifact.

43
00:03:29,175 --> 00:03:30,379
The other options.

44
00:03:30,379 --> 00:03:34,191
Native image starts in milliseconds and uses less memory.

45
00:03:34,191 --> 00:03:46,093
But peak throughput is usually lower, builds take minutes, and it's a closed world: anything found at runtime, like reflection or dynamic proxies, must be declared at build time.

46
00:03:46,093 --> 00:03:52,045
Choose it when startup is the product requirement, like serverless or command-line tools.

47
00:03:52,045 --> 00:03:55,522
Not to save a few seconds on a long-running service.

48
00:03:55,972 --> 00:04:03,131
CRaC, checkpoint and restore, snapshots a warmed-up JVM and restores it later, already at peak.

49
00:04:03,131 --> 00:04:07,352
It's the only option that skips both startup and warmup.

50
00:04:07,352 --> 00:04:16,772
The cost is coordination: open files, sockets and pools can't survive a snapshot, so you need hooks to close and reopen them.

51
00:04:17,222 --> 00:04:23,907
And for a JVM that lives a few seconds, stop at the C1 compiler and use Serial GC.

52
00:04:23,907 --> 00:04:30,022
Right for CLI tools and CI steps, clearly wrong for a long-running service.

53
00:04:30,922 --> 00:04:32,358
So how do you decide?

54
00:04:32,358 --> 00:04:34,819
First: does startup actually matter?

55
00:04:34,819 --> 00:04:38,307
For a service restarted during a weekly deploy, no.

56
00:04:38,307 --> 00:04:39,059
Do nothing.

57
00:04:39,059 --> 00:04:41,589
That's the branch most often skipped.

58
00:04:42,039 --> 00:04:47,735
For a cheap twenty to fifty percent improvement, use AppCDS and the AOT cache.

59
00:04:48,185 --> 00:04:52,622
If startup in milliseconds is the product requirement, native image.

60
00:04:53,072 --> 00:04:56,955
If you need milliseconds and full peak throughput, CRaC.

61
00:04:57,405 --> 00:05:01,458
And for processes that live only seconds, stop at C1.

62
00:05:02,358 --> 00:05:03,396
Two internals.

63
00:05:03,396 --> 00:05:16,377
Class loading is slow mostly because of verification: proving each method's bytecode is type-safe is a real dataflow analysis, and with twelve thousand classes that's seconds.

64
00:05:16,377 --> 00:05:21,643
CDS stores classes already verified, so loading becomes memory-mapping.

65
00:05:22,093 --> 00:05:25,576
And why not just compile everything ahead of time?

66
00:05:25,576 --> 00:05:34,629
Because C2's best optimisations depend on runtime profiles: which branch is taken, which implementation a call site actually sees.

67
00:05:34,629 --> 00:05:39,783
Compile blind and you lose devirtualisation and the inlining that follows.

68
00:05:39,783 --> 00:05:48,419
That's why native image trades peak throughput, and why recording real profiles, as Leyden does, is the more promising path.

69
00:05:49,319 --> 00:05:50,668
Production rules.

70
00:05:50,668 --> 00:05:53,367
First ask whether startup matters.

71
00:05:53,367 --> 00:05:56,066
Measure with the startup time log.

72
00:05:56,066 --> 00:06:00,035
Use AppCDS with auto-create, which is nearly free.

73
00:06:00,035 --> 00:06:06,068
Build archives and AOT caches in CI or the image, never by hand on a server.

74
00:06:06,068 --> 00:06:09,799
And train the AOT cache on a real request path.

75
00:06:10,249 --> 00:06:15,786
Never move a long-running service to native image just to start faster.

76
00:06:15,786 --> 00:06:19,140
Never reuse an archive across JDK versions.

77
00:06:19,140 --> 00:06:21,480
Never stop at C1 on a service.

78
00:06:21,480 --> 00:06:27,251
And don't tune JVM startup when your own initialisation is what dominates.

79
00:06:28,151 --> 00:06:28,794
To recap.

80
00:06:28,794 --> 00:06:31,724
Startup and warmup are different numbers.

81
00:06:31,724 --> 00:06:36,654
CDS and the AOT cache make startup faster without changing behaviour.

82
00:06:36,654 --> 00:06:43,298
Native image starts in milliseconds, at the cost of a closed world and lower peak throughput.

83
00:06:43,298 --> 00:06:47,799
And CRaC skips both startup and warmup, with some coordination.

84
00:06:48,249 --> 00:06:52,638
Next is chapter thirty-seven: profiling and Java Flight Recorder.

85
00:06:52,638 --> 00:06:53,988
Thanks for watching.
